<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pripet Marshes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pinsk Marshes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pripet Marshes are a vast area of forests, wetlands and rivers along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pripyat-river">Pripyat River</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, among the largest wetlands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Long a barrier to armies and a refuge for their inhabitants, they formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. They divided the fronts of the German invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pripet-marshes.jpg" alt="Pripet Marshes" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Niedźwiedzki · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prypiat_River_in_the_Chernobyl_Exclusion_Zone,_May_2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Pripet Marshes, also called the Pripyat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinsk">Pinsk</a>, Polesie or Rokitno Marshes, lie in Polesia along the forested basin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pripyat-river">Pripyat River</a> and its tributaries, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a> in the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mogilev">Mogilev</a> in the northeast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> in the southeast. Most of the region is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and part is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. They cover roughly 269,400 square kilometres and extend about 480 kilometres from west to east and 225 kilometres from north to south. The marshes change greatly in size through the year, since melting snow in spring and autumn rain cause extensive flooding as the river overflows.</p><p>Dense woods are interspersed with marshes, moors, ponds and streams. Open sedge and reed spaces alternate with almost impenetrable shrub thickets, and on dry islands there are deciduous or pine forests. In the spring flood the swamps are almost completely covered with water, and local people often cross them by boat. The region has 827 species of vascular plants and 246 recorded bird species, and it is a wintering area for migratory birds that nest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, the Baltic states and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For most of the year the marshes were virtually impassable to large military forces, which shaped strategic planning in the region. Like most other wetlands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, they were once regarded as unhealthy and a source of sickness. Drainage of the eastern part began in 1872, and by the late 19th century it had destroyed 1.5 million hectares of wetland for pasture and farmland.</p><p>At the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the marshes separated the Austro-Hungarian Fourth Army from the XII corps. The Third Army Corps of the Imperial Russian Army entered the gap before the Second Army's transfer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> was complete, and the Russians soon captured the railhead at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lemberg</a>, capital of Austrian Galicia. The wetlands remained one of the principal obstacles on the Eastern Front for the rest of the war.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> they divided the central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and sheltered Soviet and Polish partisans. German armies invading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> skirted them to the north or south, and German troops dreaded them. In 1942 about 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> escaped from the Łachwa Ghetto after an uprising, and about 600 of them found refuge in the marshes. The German administration planned the Pripet Plan, led by Konrad Meyer, to drain the marshes, remove their inhabitants and settle German colonists, but Hitler cancelled it late in 1941, fearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dust-bowl">Dust Bowl</a> conditions. A plan to drain the wetlands was also made in 1952 under Soviet administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Pripet Marshes are the largest wetland area in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The Chernobyl exclusion zone lies about 356 kilometres east-southeast of their geographic centre, and the town of Pripyat is not to be confused with the marshes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pripet Marshes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
